														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2307945">
									

<p>I like it, and have upvoted. I have a few nitpicks though:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;See&nbsp;them</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Aaagh,&nbsp;now&nbsp;I&nbsp;can't&nbsp;unsee&nbsp;them</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>1) The containment procedures don't actually say how they're contained. &quot;at Site-18<span style="text-decoration: underline;">, in a standard plastic insect viewing box</span>&quot; would be enough.</p>
<p>2) You always refer to them as &quot;the objects&quot; which sounded like they were non-living. Suggest using &quot;the insects&quot;.</p>
<p>3) As Agent MacLeod said, I found the verb form changes disorienting. By the end of the first paragraph, I thought that they were dead, and wondered why this wasn't classed as Neutralized. I think it would help to mention that we're talking about multiple generations up-front, and perhaps moving the paragraph on discovery towards the end of the entry, after describing the behaviour seen in testing.</p>
<p>4)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2616 had long memory spans, and were capable of solving advanced problems, such as navigating mazes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This rest of the entry suggests that only SCP-2616-A is capable of solving problems, and that -B is not. Query whether this sentence is necessary at all?</p>
<p>5)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During containment the objects preferred to be together.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels like too much of an intuitive leap for the scientific method. I suggest sticking to the description of behaviour later in the paragraph, and letting the reader draw their own conclusions.</p>
<p>6)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2616-A observed SCP-2616-B deposit its larvae and mimicked this behavior after an extended time period.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't this be the other way around? In all of the other examples, -B learns from -A or -A protects -B. Am I missing something?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

